  "worked_solution": "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14225859/assets/0938d12d93c4f962a4ebd8daa7efe4543fc30c419be7d89f165e21bcadce84bb.png)\n\nThe upper sum $U(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nU(f,P)={M}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{M}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{M}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{M}_{22}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{22}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${M}_{ij}$ is the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *maximum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *upper* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=8$ for all $4$ subregions, the formula for $U(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & =8\\cdot {M}_{11}+8\\cdot {M}_{21}+8\\cdot {M}_{12}+8\\cdot {M}_{22} \\\\  & =8({M}_{11}+{M}_{21}+{M}_{12}+{M}_{22}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)=x+y$ increases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=x+y$ increases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14225859/assets/af324138b21609cef59f49df0920ecc12681b8cb7667a843ff2d3fff3c27ea13.png)\n\nAs a result, the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *largest* possible $x$ and the *largest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *top right corner* of each subregion. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14225859/assets/14b50472bce6d935803ca4f74bb5b1761903c0b0b015ce72e7e1e5e08f35868b.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{11} & =f(6,5)=11 \\\\ {M}_{21} & =f(10,5)=15 \\\\ {M}_{12} & =f(6,7)=13 \\\\ {M}_{22} & =f(10,7)=17\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & =8(11+15+13+17) \\\\  & =448.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
